Q: Kernighan and Ritchie book. If the string does not contain a newline character, then the last character of the string will be removed. Is this a bug? A: <p>There are problems in function <code>mygetline</code>:</p>
<ul>
<li>the code has undefined behavior if <code>lim &lt;= 1</code> as <code>c</code> is uninitialized and may happen to have the value <code>'\n'</code> which will cause 2 bytes to be written to the buffer, beyond the end specified by <code>lim</code>.</li>
<li>note also that lines with exactly <code>lim-1</code> bytes before the newline will be broken in 2 chunks, which is counter-intuitive.</li>
</ul>
<p>Note that the <code>getline</code> function presented in K&amp;R page 30 is written differently but has the same problems:</p>
<pre><code>/* getline: read a line into s, return length */
int getline(char s[], int lim)
{
    int c, i;
    for (i = 0; i &lt; lim - 1 &amp;&amp; (c = getchar()) != EOF &amp;&amp; c != '\n'; ++i)
        s[i] = c;
    if (c == '\n') {
        s[i] = c;
        ++i;
    }
    s[i] = '\0';
    return i;
}
</code></pre>
<p><code>readlines</code> has its own set of issues:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>line[len - 1] = '\0'</code> will indeed overwrite the last byte in the buffer, either on the last line if it does not end with a newline in the file, but also on all fragments of lines longer than <code>MAXLEN-1</code>. This is a bug.</li>
<li>truncated line chunks should be concatenated into a reallocated buffer, otherwise they will appear as separate lines, which is incorrect and my lead to unexpected behavior.</li>
<li>memory is not freed in case the file is longer than <code>maxlines</code> or in case of memory allocation failure.  This is not a problem if memory is allocated with the <code>alloc</code> function presented in the K&amp;R book, but should be fixed if the memory were allocated with <code>malloc</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Here is a modified version:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stddef.h&gt;
#include &quot;mylib.h&quot;

#define MAXLEN 1000

void freelines(char *lineptr[], int maxlines) {
    while (maxlines-- &gt; 0)
        myfree(lineptr[lineptr]);
}

int readlines(char *lineptr[], int maxlines) {
    char line[MAXLEN];
    char *p;
    int len;
    int nlines = 0;
    int concatenate_len = 0;
    while ((len = mygetline(line, MAXLEN)) &gt; 0) {
        // strip the trailing newline if present
        int has_newline = (line[len - 1] == '\n');
        if (has_newline) {
            line[--len] = '\0';
        }
        if (concatenate_len) {
            if ((p = alloc(concatenate_len + len + 1)) == NULL) {
                freelines(lineptr, nlines);
                return -1;
            }
            mystrcpy(p, lineptr[nlines - 1]);
            mystrcpy(p + concatenate_len, line);
            myfree(lineptr[nlines - 1]);
            lineptr[nlines - 1] = p;
        } else {
            if (nlines &gt;= maxlines || (p = alloc(len + 1)) == NULL) {
                freelines(lineptr, nlines);
                return -1;
            }
            mystrcpy(p, line);
            lineptr[nlines++] = p;
        }
        concatenate_len = has_newline ? 0 : concatenate_len + len;
    }
    return nlines;
}
</code></pre>

Q: ODBC must declare the scalar variable A: <p>In ODBC Driver 18, SQL Server 2022, using a variable name after the question mark didn't work for me. So the above code would be</p>
<pre><code>string qryStr = &quot;insert into info(code) values(?);&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>in order for the insert to be executed. (This solution was posted <a href="https://community.progress.com/s/article/P176215#:%7E:text=How%20do%20I%20do%20parameterized%20SQL%20queries%20in%20C%23?,14/2017%2012:44%20PM" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>.) In my case I had several fields, so my statement looked like</p>
<pre><code>var insertQry = new StringBuilder(&quot;INSERT INTO books (&quot;);
insertQry.Append(&quot;author, &quot;);
insertQry.Append(&quot;title, &quot;);
insertQry.Append(&quot;genre, &quot;);
insertQry.Append(&quot;price, &quot;);
insertQry.Append(&quot;description, &quot;);
insertQry.Append(&quot;Values(?, ?, ?, ?, ?)&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>and parameterizing as usual with</p>
<pre><code>using (var conn = new OdbcConnection(connStr))
{
    conn.Open();
    foreach(Book book in books)
    {        
        using (var cmd = new OdbcCommand(insertQry.ToString(), conn))
        {
            cmd.Parameters.Add(&quot;@author&quot;, SqlDbType.VarChar).Value = book.author;
            cmd.Parameters.Add(&quot;@title&quot;, SqlDbType.Char).Value = book.title;
            cmd.Parameters.Add(&quot;@genre&quot;, SqlDbType.Char).Value = book.genre;
            cmd.Parameters.Add(&quot;@price&quot;, SqlDbType.Char).Value = book.price;
            cmd.Parameters.Add(&quot;@description&quot;, SqlDbType.Char).Value = book.description;
            cmd.ExecuteNonQuery();
    }
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: ComposePausableCompositionException and IllegalStateException: Apply is called on deactivated node when fast scrolling LazyColumn with Coil AsyncImage A: <h3>**Solution To The Problem :</h3>
<p>The Problem and Diagnosis**</p>
<p>When using Coil's <code>AsyncImage</code> within a <code>LazyColumn</code> in Jetpack Compose, the application may crash under rapid scrolling conditions with the following errors:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><code>androidx.compose.runtime.ComposePausableCompositionException: Failed to execute op number 24</code></p>
</li>
<li><p><code>java.lang.IllegalStateException: Apply is called on deactivated node</code></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Diagnostic Process:</strong> To isolate the root cause, I performed systematic trial-and-error testing. I discovered that removing <code>AsyncImage</code> and replacing it with a standard <code>Box</code> or <code>Image</code> completely eliminated the crashes. This proved the issue stems directly from <code>AsyncImage</code>'s internal rendering and measurement mechanics.</p>
<h3><strong>The Root Cause: <code>ConstraintsSizeResolver</code> and Race Condition</strong></h3>
<p>The crash is rooted in how Coil's internal <code>ConstraintsSizeResolver</code> class handles asynchronous size resolution.</p>
<h4><strong>1. ConstraintsSizeResolver and MutableStateFlow</strong></h4>
<p>Coil uses a <code>LayoutModifier</code> called <code>ConstraintsSizeResolver</code> to determine the image size, tracking layout constraints via a <code>MutableStateFlow</code>:</p>
<pre><code>internal class ConstraintsSizeResolver : SizeResolver, LayoutModifier {
    private val _constraints = MutableStateFlow(ZeroConstraints)

    // Flow'un ilk geçerli değeri üretmesini bekler (Asenkron)
    override suspend fun size() = _constraints.mapNotNull(Constraints::toSizeOrNull).first()

    override fun MeasureScope.measure(measurable: Measurable, constraints: Constraints): MeasureResult {
        // Cache the current constraints.
        _constraints.value = constraints
        val placeable = measurable.measure(constraints)
        return layout(placeable.width, placeable.height) { placeable.place(0, 0) }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h4><strong>2. Race Condition and Deactivated Node</strong></h4>
<p>During rapid scrolling, <code>LazyColumn</code> immediately <strong>&quot;deactivates&quot;</strong> (recycles) <code>LayoutNodes</code> that leave the screen to maintain performance. However, Coil's <code>size()</code> function may still be waiting for the <code>Flow</code> to emit the initial size or attempting to apply changes when the node is already <strong>deactivated</strong>.</p>
<p>The error <code>Caused by: java.lang.IllegalStateException: Apply is called on deactivated node</code> indicates a race condition. Compose runtime throws this exception to prevent a safe-critical state when Coil's asynchronous flow tries to apply changes to a disposed or deactivated node.</p>
<hr />
<h3><strong>Solution Scenarios</strong></h3>
<p>This issue can be mitigated using one of three strategies:</p>
<h4><strong>1) Use <code>rememberAsyncImagePainter</code> + <code>Image</code> (Recommended)</strong></h4>
<p>This is the most stable and performant solution for fast-scrolling lists. By using <code>rememberAsyncImagePainter</code>, you entirely bypass <code>AsyncImage</code>'s complex subcomposition and asynchronous flow-based constraint resolution mechanics.</p>
<h4><strong>2) Set <code>ContentScale.None</code></strong></h4>
<p>If you analyze Coil's <code>updateRequest</code> method, setting <code>contentScale</code> to <code>None</code> forces Coil to use <code>SizeResolver(CoilSize.ORIGINAL)</code> (fixed size). This prevents the asynchronous <code>ConstraintsSizeResolver</code> from ever being instantiated:</p>
<pre><code>@Composable
internal fun updateRequest(request: ImageRequest, contentScale: ContentScale): ImageRequest {
    return if (request.defined.sizeResolver == null) {
        val sizeResolver = if (contentScale == ContentScale.None) {
            SizeResolver(CoilSize.ORIGINAL) 
        } else {
            remember { ConstraintsSizeResolver() } // The risky one
        }
        request.newBuilder().size(sizeResolver).build()
    } else {
        request // Safe if sizeResolver is already defined
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h4><strong>3) Define Manuel <code>.size()</code> in <code>ImageRequest</code></strong></h4>
<p>If you define a fixed size in your <code>ImageRequest</code> (e.g., <code>.size(200)</code>), Coil already knows the target size. As shown in the code block above, the <code>if (request.defined.sizeResolver == null)</code> check will fail, and it will directly enter the safe <code>else { request }</code> block, entirely bypassing the flawed asynchronous calculation process.</p>

Q: RecyclerView horizontal scrolling to left A: <p>Set RecyclerView layout layoutDirection LTR:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;androidx.recyclerview.widget.RecyclerView
    android:id=&quot;@+id/recyclerView&quot;
    android:layout_width=&quot;match_parent&quot;
    android:layout_height=&quot;wrap_content&quot;
    android:layoutDirection=&quot;ltr&quot; /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>And set</p>
<pre><code>val layoutManager = LinearLayoutManager(this,LinearLayoutManager.HORIZONTAL, true)
</code></pre>
